    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1169\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to mandatory\nseat belt use\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo require the use of seat belts by all passengers in a motor vehicle\nriding in both the front and rear seats.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe institution of a mandatory seat belt law for all front seat\npassengers has saved lives. According to the New York State Department\nof Motor Vehicles, in 1984, the year the seat belt law was enacted,\napproximately 16% of individuals wore seat belts.  Also, one thousand\ntwelve (1,012) people who were unrestrained died in motor vehicle\ncrashes. Just one year after the law was enacted, the compliance rate\nwas up to 57% of individuals and unrestrained fatalities fell to 644.\nA little over 10 years later, the compliance rate was up to 73%.\n\nIn studies done by the United States Department of Transportation,\napproximately 52% of rear seat passengers do not use safety belts.\nRecognizing the serious injuries that may be caused to other\npassengers or the driver of a motor vehicle by an unrestrained\noccupant of the vehicle, 14 states have adopted legislation requiring\nall rear seat occupants to wear safety restraints.\n\nExtensive research has shown that unbelted rear seat passengers can\nkill and injure others. An unrestrained adult in the back seat of a\nmotor vehicle involved in a 30 mph car crash can strike a restrained\nfront seat occupant with the equivalent weight of a 3 1/2 ton truck.\n\nAlso, an individual has a 7 times greater chance of being ejected from\nthe rear seat of a vehicle than the front seat, 18% of motor vehicle\naccident fatalities result from rear seat ejection. As a result,\nsafety experts believe that the use of a rear seat belt could prevent\nover 2/3rds of fatalities and serious injuries resulting from motor\nvehicle accidents.\n\nTherefore, this legislation seeks to reduce automobile accident\nfatalities and casualties by requiring all occupants of a motor\nvehicle to buckle up.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection 1: Subdivision 1 of section 1229-c of the vehicle and traffic\nlaw, as amended by chapter 410 of the laws of 2000 is amended to\nrequire the use of seat belts by all passengers in a motor vehicle in\nboth the front and rear seats.\n\nSection 2. Effective Date\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2002 (A.11063-A/S.6302-A)\n2003-04 (A.903/S.118)\n2005-2006 (A.2157-A/S.3582-A)\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone known.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe act shall take effect on the first of November next succeeding the\ndate on which it shall have become a law.\n",
